bromure-ac for Debian/Ubuntu
============================

After installing the package:

1. Storage: `sudo bromure-setup-environment`. Puts /opt/bromure on btrfs
   (a loop image when the disk isn't btrfs) and the server's state and
   guest image on it: workspace disks, clones and checkpoints become
   reflinks. Moving existing data needs the server stopped; it says how.
2. Guest image: `sudo bromure-image-install` (downloads the prebuilt image,
   or bakes it here; needs /dev/kvm).
3. Users: `sudo bromure-ac admin add --existing <login>` for someone with
   a login here (`admin add <name>` makes a system account instead).
4. Each user signs the server in to their bromure.io account:
   `bromure-ac account sign-in` (for a system account,
   `sudo -u <name> bromure-ac account sign-in`). It prints a link to approve
   in a browser. The Bromure app on a Mac, iPhone or iPad, signed in to the
   same account, then lists this server: no address or port to type.
   On the same network the app can also add the server by address, port
   2222 (/var/lib/bromure-gw/gateway.json), with a one-time code from
   `sudo bromure-ac admin code <name>`.

Files: /usr/lib/bromure-ac (binary, Swift runtime, Firecracker, PII model,
resources), units in /usr/lib/systemd/system, state in /var/lib/bromure-ac.
See RELEASE_NOTES.md beside this file.
